WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.170
Think of the first human being who drew a fence around a field and said, "this place is mine." Saying that sentence was not enough; the real matter was that the people facing him believed it.

2
00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:17.220
So if you made none of the things in your hands yourself, what exactly is it that says they are yours?

3
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.470
Picture a field.

4
00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:30.800
Early morning, the dew still on the grass.

5
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.370
A man comes walking, a bundle of stakes on his shoulder, a mallet in his hand.

6
00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:41.460
He stops, looks around him, then drives the first stake into the ground.

7
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.470
The sound of the mallet echoes through the valley.

8
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:47.480
Then a second stake, then a third.

9
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.890
By evening a rope is stretched around the crooked quadrangle he has traced with his own footsteps.

10
00:00:52.890 --> 00:01:02.460
And the man straightens up, wipes the sweat from his face, turns to the neighbours who happen to be passing and says, in the most ordinary voice in the world: this is mine.

11
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.470
This is the strangest moment in history.

12
00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:08.960
Because that sentence rests on nothing at all.

13
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.810
The land did not come out of his body.

14
00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.260
He did not buy it from anyone, because buying had not yet been invented.

15
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.150
Nobody gave it to him.

16
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.480
The man only stretched a rope and spoke a sentence.

17
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.170
And the sentence hung there in the air.

18
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.860
What matters is what happens next.

19
00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.870
What did the crowd do?

20
00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:34.000
Did they laugh, tear out the stakes, drive the man off?

21
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.890
No.

22
00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:40.980
The crowd stopped, looked, looked at one another, and stayed outside the rope.

23
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:51.990
In his famous text of seventeen fifty-five, the Discourse on the Origin of Inequality, Rousseau stages precisely this scene, and he turns its sharp end not on the man but on the crowd.

24
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:58.200
The true founder of civil society is not the one who puts up the fence; it is those who agree to believe him.

25
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:09.210
Had someone stepped forward, pulled up the stakes and said the land belongs to no one, Rousseau writes, humanity would have been spared who knows how many wars, how many murders, how much misery.

26
00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.580
This may sound like a fable.

27
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.950
It is not.

28
00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:22.080
The same scene, with stone walls and lines of thorn hedge in place of the rope, was played out for centuries in the English countryside.

29
00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:31.330
There was such a thing as common land: the village pasture open to everyone, the coppice where firewood was gathered, the fallow field where animals grazed.

30
00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:38.180
You were not rich, but if you had a cow you had somewhere to graze it, and you had brush to feed your stove in winter.

31
00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:41.830
Then these lands were enclosed, one by one.

32
00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:50.760
Across the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, through thousands of separate acts of parliament, the commons were surveyed, divided, bounded and registered.

33
00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.570
It was legal, it was orderly, it was on the record.

34
00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:59.500
Only one thing happened: the person with nowhere to graze a cow sold the cow.

35
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.950
The person who sold the cow had to sell his labour instead.

36
00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.560
That crowd walking from countryside to city gathered at the factory gates.

37
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.210
A fence does not only cut land in two.

38
00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.700
It cuts people as well.

39
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:22.070
The one left inside and the one left outside are born, in the same instant, as two separate classes.

40
00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:27.800
And the cruellest form of the fence is the one that behaves as though it had never been put up at all.

41
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:33.210
European jurists used a concept for centuries: land that belongs to no one.

42
00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:44.860
Even when people were living on it, if those people had not divided that land with fences the way Europeans did, had not entered it in a register, had not ploughed and sown it, the land was counted as empty.

43
00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:49.470
The continent of Australia was governed on that assumption for centuries.

44
00:03:49.470 --> 00:04:00.960
Not until nineteen ninety-two did the country's highest court reject it, in the decision known as the Mabo case, recognising that the bond indigenous peoples had with land also gave rise to a right.

45
00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:08.930
For nearly two hundred years, in other words, the law said: this is not your land, because you did not build the fence that we build.

46
00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:12.260
Well then, what if the fence had never gone up?

47
00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:22.630
In nineteen sixty-eight a biologist, Garrett Hardin, wrote a famous scenario: a common pasture with no owner inevitably collapses, because each person adds one more animal.

48
00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:26.760
For a long time this was taken as the decisive defence of property.

49
00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:36.170
Until Elinor Ostrom spent years examining real pastures, real fishing villages, real irrigation canals, field by field, all over the world.

50
00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:46.060
The study Ostrom published in nineteen ninety, and the Nobel prize she received in two thousand nine, showed this: there are commons that have worked for centuries without collapsing.

51
00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:49.390
What holds them up is neither the state nor a title deed.

52
00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:55.920
It is people who make their own rules, who police violations themselves, who have to look one another in the face.

53
00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.290
So the fence was not inevitable.

54
00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.340
It was a choice.

55
00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.990
And like every choice, it demands a justification.

56
00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:10.360
The man who built that justification more effectively than anyone in history wrote some seventy years before Rousseau.

57
00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:21.690
In the second of his Two Treatises of Government, published in sixteen eighty-nine, John Locke sets out to solve the question of property at the root, and he begins from somewhere marvellously simple: from your body.

58
00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.220
Locke's starting point is this.

59
00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:27.710
In the beginning the earth belongs to all of us, true.

60
00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:32.320
But there is exactly one thing that is indisputably yours: your own body.

61
00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:34.370
Your hands are yours.

62
00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.700
Therefore the work of your hands is yours as well.

63
00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:44.710
When the man walking in the forest bends down beneath the oak and gathers acorns, those acorns have left the common store.

64
00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:48.520
He asked no one's permission, consulted nobody.

65
00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.210
He only bent down.

66
00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:55.980
He joined his labour to nature, and where he joined it, nature became his.

67
00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:01.070
The power of this idea lies in the fact that it still sits in every one of our instincts.

68
00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:10.800
If you dug a garden for three years, picked the stones out of it yourself, planted the trees yourself, and in the spring of the fourth year somebody arrives and says this is mine,

69
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.770
the thing that snaps inside you is exactly what Locke described.

70
00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:18.740
Labour is a piece a person tears off from himself.

71
00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.870
When you join it to something, you leave part of yourself there.

72
00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:26.360
But Locke is not as comfortable a man as we assume.

73
00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.850
He immediately lays two heavy conditions on this right.

74
00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:34.460
The first: take as much as you can use, do not let it spoil.

75
00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:40.030
If the fruit you gathered is rotting in your hands, that fruit was never yours; you wasted it.

76
00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:46.400
The second, and the more searing: enough must be left behind for others, and left as good as what you took.

77
00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:51.650
Which means that if your fence leaves your neighbour without water, that fence is not legitimate.

78
00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.700
Now pay attention.

79
00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:59.910
Locke himself also explains how these two conditions came to be overcome: by the invention of money.

80
00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:07.560
The moment you begin to accumulate gold that does not rot instead of apples that do, the first condition becomes meaningless.

81
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.370
You can accumulate without limit and nothing spoils.

82
00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:20.300
And once limitless accumulation begins, the second condition collapses of its own accord, because enough for everyone is no longer left behind.

83
00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:26.190
Locke tells this not as a catastrophe but as an arrangement to which people quietly consented.

84
00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:33.680
Yet what he is admitting is this: that clean justification resting on labour has been worn away by the very order that invokes it.

85
00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:36.690
Objections came too.

86
00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:45.140
The sharpest of them is unforgettable because it is so plain: is joining your labour to something a way of gaining that thing, or of losing your labour?

87
00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:49.270
Robert Nozick asks it in nineteen seventy-four.

88
00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:54.200
You have a can of tomato juice and you pour it into the ocean.

89
00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.370
Your tomato juice has mixed with the sea.

90
00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:02.620
Has the ocean now become yours, or have you merely lost your tomato juice?

91
00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:10.910
Joining your labour, then, generates a right not in proportion to the size of what you joined it to, but according to whether everyone consents to it.

92
00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:14.400
The justification comes back to the crowd again.

93
00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.250
There was a harsher voice as well.

94
00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:25.700
In What Is Property?, published in eighteen forty, Pierre-Joseph Proudhon answers his own question in three words: property is theft.

95
00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:29.670
But what Proudhon means is not your house or your tools.

96
00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:41.800
The distinction he draws is this: property you use, you work, you live inside is one thing; property you never touch, that you own only on paper and through which you take a share of someone else's labour,

97
00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:44.010
is quite another.

98
00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.180
To the first he raises no objection.

99
00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:49.390
The second he calls theft.

100
00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:53.040
And there is a distinction that sits at the very centre of the matter.

101
00:08:53.040 --> 00:09:01.490
In eighteen seventy-nine Henry George sees it: if you made a table, the table is yours, because before you made it the table did not exist.

102
00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:04.340
But the land was there before you were born.

103
00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:06.870
You did not produce the land.

104
00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:16.920
The value of a plot arises from nothing its owner did, but from a road being laid past it, a school opening nearby, that is, from the collective labour of others.

105
00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:27.130
This is why George argued for a tax on land itself: let whatever you produced remain with you, and let the value the land gains thanks to all of us return to all of us.

106
00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:30.140
The labour justification is strong.

107
00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:32.670
But left on its own it is not enough.

108
00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:39.040
Because labour cannot say how far it reaches, when it ends, or how it is passed on through inheritance.

109
00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.210
Another framework is needed to say that.

110
00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:50.980
There is a tradition of thought that builds that framework from a place very far from the Western debate on property, and in building it, it makes one very basic move:

111
00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:55.590
it takes ownership out of human hands and sets it one level higher.

112
00:09:55.590 --> 00:10:02.600
The phrase repeated in various places in the Qur'an is plain: to God belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth.

113
00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:10.730
This is not a legal sentence but a sentence about existence itself; it takes the topmost link of the chain of ownership out of human hands.

114
00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:18.860
In this way of thinking, the human position is not that of malik, the owner, but of khalifa, the steward, and of trustee.

115
00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:22.830
What has been given to you is not property but the authority to use.

116
00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:27.920
It is yours because you hold it; you do not hold it because it is yours.

117
00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:31.570
This distinction may sound like a subtle point of theology.

118
00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:35.060
But its practical consequences are very concrete.

119
00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:44.150
A saying attributed to the Prophet and recorded in the hadith collections declares that people are partners in three things: water, pasture and fire.

120
00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:47.960
That is, the spring, the grazing ground and the fuel.

121
00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:55.450
These cannot be fenced, because they are not things brought into being by anyone's labour; they are the conditions of life itself.

122
00:10:55.450 --> 00:10:57.980
So how does land become yours?

123
00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:03.710
The answer Islamic law gives to this question is ihya al-mawat, the revival of dead land.

124
00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:13.760
Whoever revives an ownerless, uncultivated, dead piece of ground, whoever clears its stones and brings water to it and makes it fit for planting, that ground becomes his.

125
00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:17.570
This stands surprisingly close to Locke's idea of labour.

126
00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:25.380
But with one critical difference: here the right arises not from labour being joined to something, but from the land being brought to life.

127
00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:28.870
The measure is not in you; it is in the soil.

128
00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:34.600
If you do not revive it, if you cannot revive it, the right lapses the moment you let go.

129
00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:38.570
Ownership here is not a possession but the counterpart of a duty.

130
00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:41.900
The ripest fruit of this logic is the waqf.

131
00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:49.710
According to the account, Umar acquires a valuable piece of land at Khaybar and comes to the Prophet to ask what he should do with it.

132
00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:54.960
The answer he receives is short: hold back the substance, give away the yield.

133
00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:01.330
The land is made unsellable, un-inheritable, un-giftable; only its produce keeps flowing.

134
00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:08.820
That is exactly what a waqf is, and it is a strange thing: property that has a holder but that nobody can ever own.

135
00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:19.190
Across Ottoman lands, soup kitchens, madrasas, bridges, fountains and caravanserais stood for centuries on this legal invention.

136
00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:26.040
The person who draws up the waqf deed does not lose his property; he freezes it, and declares himself its servant.

137
00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:29.850
And this is where the answer to our question emerges.

138
00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:32.540
Is ownership a burden?

139
00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:34.910
In this tradition, yes.

140
00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:39.200
For property to be yours means being answerable for what you do with it.

141
00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:41.890
A trust is a weight that has to be carried.

142
00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:46.180
The Ottoman land order applied this logic at the scale of a state.

143
00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:54.470
Most arable land counted as miri: the bare ownership, the raqaba, belonged to the state, while the right of use belonged to the peasant.

144
00:12:54.470 --> 00:13:03.080
In the sixteenth century the Şeyhülislam Ebussuud Efendi systematised this order in law and defined the title in the peasant's hands as a kind of lease.

145
00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:09.130
The peasant could not sell the land, could not divide it as he pleased, could not leave it idle at will.

146
00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:16.460
Land left unsown for three years running without valid excuse became mahlul: it was taken from him and given to someone else.

147
00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:20.910
The price of keeping land in your hands, in other words, was sowing it.

148
00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:24.560
The way this system came apart is instructive too.

149
00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:30.450
With the Land Code of eighteen fifty-eight, land began to be registered in the names of individuals.

150
00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:34.420
But a title deed also meant taxes and conscription.

151
00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:43.990
Many peasants were afraid to have their names entered in the register, and had their land recorded in the name of a tribal chief, an influential merchant, some local notable they knew.

152
00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:47.160
On paper the land was theirs.

153
00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:50.970
After a while, the paper became the reality itself.

154
00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:56.060
Those who had tilled that soil for centuries became tenants of the very fields they sowed.

155
00:13:56.060 --> 00:14:00.190
A fence had been drawn not by driving stakes, but with ink.

156
00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:04.480
Ibn Khaldun had seen this long before, in the Muqaddimah.

157
00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:09.090
For him, the surest sign of a state's collapse is hands reaching for people's property.

158
00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:20.260
Because the moment a person understands that what he produces will not stay with him, he stops producing; when production stops, taxes stop, and when taxes stop, the state stops.

159
00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:24.390
Injustice ruins umran, the flourishing of settled life.

160
00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:31.400
Property appears here not as a sacred right but as a fragile promise that keeps a civilisation's willingness to work alive.

161
00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:42.730
And the question beneath this entire debate is asked in two lines by the quatrain attributed to Yunus Emre in Anatolia: owner of goods, owner of property, where then is this thing's first owner?

162
00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:48.300
Asked from the graveyard, the question of who was there before the fence went up answers itself.

163
00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:52.590
Now get up from that first man's field and come back to today.

164
00:14:52.590 --> 00:15:00.880
Because do not imagine the fence has vanished; it has only become invisible, and it now runs not around land but around far stranger things.

165
00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:03.730
Let us start with inheritance.

166
00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:10.580
If you have a house and that house came to you from your father, the fact that the house is yours has nothing whatever to do with labour.

167
00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:13.750
It is yours because you were born.

168
00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:21.560
Locke's entire justification is left hanging here: nobody's labour can be transferred by inheritance, only its result.

169
00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:28.410
What is interesting is that almost every legal tradition, while accepting this transfer, also tries to limit it.

170
00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:36.700
The Turkish Civil Code has an institution called the reserved share: you cannot erase your children's and your spouse's portion entirely by your will.

171
00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:48.830
Islamic inheritance law, the fara'id, goes further still and fixes the shares one by one, with their ratios; it makes the gathering of property into a single pair of hands impossible from the outset.

172
00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:54.080
Both systems carry the same intuition: property should disperse at death.

173
00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:59.970
Because if it does not disperse, it accumulates, and as it accumulates the fence grows.

174
00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.020
Let us come to rent.

175
00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:06.310
The rental contract is the most everyday form of the fence.

176
00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:12.040
Two people do not live in the same flat; one lives inside it, the other owns it.

177
00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:19.690
The money the occupant pays every month is paid not for the upkeep of the flat, but for the fact that the flat is registered in somebody else's name.

178
00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:25.420
This does not mean it is a bad thing; the owner may well have worked for years to save that money.

179
00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:36.590
But Henry George's distinction rings out again right here: if the value of that flat doubled in the last five years, did the owner do that, or was it the metro line, the school,

180
00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:39.440
the hospital that opened in the neighbourhood?

181
00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:45.010
The tenant is in fact paying a value all of us produced together to a single name.

182
00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:47.220
Look at copyright.

183
00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:49.270
You wrote a song.

184
00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:57.560
The song is yours, nobody disputes it; it is the purest Lockean example, because before you wrote it that melody did not exist.

185
00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:00.730
But how long should the protection last?

186
00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:05.820
Under the rules in force in Turkey, seventy years after the death of the author.

187
00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:08.030
After death.

188
00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:19.200
Your labour, in other words, outlives you by nearly another human lifetime, and throughout that time grandchildren you never met, or a company that bought the rights, stand inside that fence.

189
00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:22.370
And when the term runs out, what happens?

190
00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:31.140
The copyright on the nineteen twenty-eight animated short film Steamboat Willie expired in the United States on the first of January, two thousand twenty-four,

191
00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:35.750
and the first drawing of Mickey Mouse inside it passed into the public domain.

192
00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:42.280
For decades it had been one of the most fiercely guarded images in the world; one morning it belonged to everyone.

193
00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:46.090
When the term ends, the fence comes down by itself.

194
00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:49.420
Which means it was never part of nature.

195
00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:52.910
It was always a calendar.

196
00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:56.080
And the last one, the quietest.

197
00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:57.970
Data.

198
00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:02.260
Today, every time you open your phone, you are ploughing a field.

199
00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:10.070
Where you went, what you looked at and for how many seconds, the hour at which you could not sleep, how many times you wrote to whom.

200
00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:14.840
You produce these traces; your labour, your attention, your life.

201
00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:18.330
But the fence around that field does not belong to you.

202
00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:29.180
Regulations such as the law on the protection of personal data that came into force in Turkey in two thousand sixteen, numbered six thousand six hundred and ninety-eight, like their counterparts in Europe,

203
00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:36.030
grant you certain rights: to request information, to have data corrected, to demand its deletion.

204
00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:39.040
Notice that every one of these is a tenant's right.

205
00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:41.250
Not the right of ownership.

206
00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:46.020
You are not the owner of the data; you are the person about whom data is kept.

207
00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:49.350
And here the true nature of the fence comes into view.

208
00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:52.840
Property is not a relationship between you and a thing.

209
00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:57.610
Nobody can have a relationship with a field; the field knows nothing of you.

210
00:18:57.610 --> 00:19:04.780
Property is a relationship between you and other people, and its content amounts to a single sentence: they will stand back.

211
00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:10.190
A title deed is not a document about land but a contract about human behaviour.

212
00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:16.080
Which is exactly why the person Rousseau really accused was not the man who put up the fence.

213
00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:18.610
Now look at your own life.

214
00:19:18.610 --> 00:19:22.260
How many fences did you stand outside today?

215
00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:28.790
A door you did not go through, a lock you did not pick, a hand you drew back as you said this is not mine.

216
00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:31.320
Nobody forced any of it upon you.

217
00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:38.810
Almost the whole of modern life stands upright because millions of people spontaneously respect boundaries they cannot see.

218
00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:42.620
This may be humanity's greatest display of good faith.

219
00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.310
It is also its greatest agreement.

220
00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:51.200
But just as every agreement has a price, it also carries a responsibility.

221
00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:56.770
The idea of trust was saying this: if it is in your hands, you are answerable for it.

222
00:19:56.770 --> 00:20:02.820
The idea of revival was saying this: if you are not bringing it to life, your right lapses.

223
00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:09.670
The idea of the waqf was saying this: the soundest ownership is the one that has bound itself to service.

224
00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:15.560
Locke's forgotten second condition was saying this: let enough be left behind for others too.

225
00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:19.530
When that man stretched his rope, the crowd fell silent.

226
00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:23.340
Thousands of years have passed and the crowd is still silent.

227
00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:26.670
But silence is not approval.

228
00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:31.760
Silence can also mean waiting for the answer to a question that has not yet been asked.

229
00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:38.290
There is no single correct answer to why this land is yours; every generation writes it again.

230
00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:44.820
Today it is you standing in that crowd, and the answer you give decides who will be left on which side of the rope tomorrow.

231
00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:48.950
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

232
00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:52.280
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
